{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And the sea returned to its strength.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): לאיתנו — 'to its strength,' from the expression of might and force, as in (Numbers 24:21) איתן מושבך — 'strong is your dwelling'; that is, to its former strength." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: לאיתנו — 'to its condition (תנאו).' And thus it hints (Genesis 1): ״ותראה היבשה״ — 'and the dry land appeared,' that is, in the waters." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, set a condition with the sea that it would split before Israel. Rabbi Eliezer said: not with the sea alone did the Holy One, blessed be He, set a condition, but with everything created in the work of Genesis, as it is said (Isaiah 45): ״אני ידי נטו שמים וכל צבאם צויתי״ — 'I, My hands stretched out the heavens, and all their host I commanded' — from the moment the heavens were stretched out, I commanded all their host: I commanded the sea that it be split before Israel; I commanded the sun and the moon that they stand before Joshua, as it is said (Joshua 10): ״שמש בגבעון דום וירח בעמק אילון״; I commanded heaven and earth that they be silent before Moses, as it is said (Deuteronomy 32): ״האזינו השמים״; I commanded the ravens to sustain Elijah, as it is said (I Kings 17): ״והעורבים מביאים לו לחם ובשר״; I commanded the fire that it not harm Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, as it is said (Daniel 3): ״וריח נור לא עדת בהון״; I commanded the lions that they not harm Daniel, as it is said (Daniel 6): ״אלהי שלח מלאכיה וסגר פום אריותא״; I commanded the fish that it vomit up Jonah, as it is said (Jonah 2): ״ויאמר ה׳ לדג״ — thus far in the Midrash of Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer. This Midrash proves that all the miracles and wonders that the Holy One, blessed be He, performed in all the generations through the prophets were already implanted during the six days of Genesis in the natures of the things created — for example, on the second day, when the sea was separated, it was implanted in the nature of water that it split before Moses at the sea, and before Joshua at the Jordan, and likewise before Elijah the prophet and before Elisha; and on the fourth day, when the sun was created, it was implanted in its nature that it stand for Joshua. According to this, there was no change of nature in any created thing from the six days of Genesis onward by way of new creation, for all the miracles and signs and wonders that were performed afterward were all already implanted in nature, and the course that changed before the eyes of beholders was not a new creation ex nihilo beyond nature. And further, the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ten things were created בין השמשות (between the suns, i.e., at twilight on the eve of the first Sabbath), and they are: the opening of the mouth of the earth, the mouth of the well, the mouth of the she-ass, the rainbow, the manna, the staff, the shamir, the writing, the inscription, and the tablets. And you should be roused to ask why these ten things in particular were singled out for בין השמשות, while all the other miracles were implanted in the natures of things made during the six preceding days. And what emerges for us from this is that from the six days of Genesis onward there is no change of nature by way of something new, for at the time that thing was created, it was implanted in its nature that it would behave thus and so by the will of the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is the sense of נבראו — 'were created' — that the Sages used: for a nature issuing from the primordial will they call 'creation' and 'condition'; and relative to the primordial will it is nature, while before the eyes of beholders it is a change of nature. And when the time arrived for the change of nature to manifest before the eyes of beholders, this does not constitute a change of will on the part of the Holy One, blessed be He, such that He now wills what He did not will at first — for so it was in His primordial will that the nature would change in its time before the eyes of beholders through the righteous of the generations." "<b>And the Egyptians fled toward it.</b> It should have said 'fled from it,' but the matter is that they were fleeing from the sea so that they would go out from it, and it came toward them against their will. This is what is said: ״וינער ה׳ את מצרים בתוך הים״ — 'and HaShem shook the Egyptians in the midst of the sea' — and the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded it as one shakes flesh inside a pot."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:21.399454292Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}